# Threads vs processes

| | Process | Thread |
|---|---|---|
| Address space | own | shared with its process |
| Creation cost | higher (new page tables, fd table copy) | low |
| Communication | pipes, sockets, shared memory | plain memory (needs synchronisation) |
| Crash isolation | one crash doesn't kill others | one bad pointer kills every thread |

Each thread has its own stack, registers and program counter; everything else is shared.

## User vs kernel threads

- Many-to-one (green threads): cheap switches, but one blocking syscall blocks all, and no parallelism.
- One-to-one (Linux, Windows): each user thread is a kernel thread.
- Many-to-many: M user threads on N kernel threads; complicated, mostly abandoned (Solaris dropped it).

## Linux detail

`clone()` with flags decides what is shared; a "thread" is a task that shares the mm, files and signal handlers. `fork` is clone with nothing shared (copy-on-write).

## Browsers

Chrome uses processes per site for isolation and threads inside each. Web workers are closest to threads without shared memory (unless you use SharedArrayBuffer, which needs cross-origin isolation).
